installation :kalkara_bake_shed do
  name "The Kalkara Bake Shed"
  tags :ecology, :military, :water
  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    A slipway, a stripping floor and three ovens on the creek at Kalkara, taking articles pulled from service by #{ref :the_fouling_round, "the round"} out of four #{ref :the_mediterranean_chain, "Chain"} enclaves. A drone comes up the slip, comes apart on the floor into pieces that fit an oven door, goes through the ovens, and goes back down the slip assembled — or does not, and its owner is told which piece stopped it.

    The shed also issues and destroys #{ref :comparison_tiles, "tile sets"}: it stamps the season onto a new case, hands sets out to named surveyors at the top of each quarter, and breaks the expired ones on a stone by the water door.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :operations, heading: "The Weighing Out"
    Pieces are weighed onto the floor and weighed again out of the oven, and both figures go in the book against the article's number. A piece that comes out lighter than the difference water accounts for has lost something else, and it is cut open on the floor rather than fitted back.

    The three ovens run at three heats. Skins, cases and fasteners go through the low oven; gate seats and bearing housings through the middle; reef anchors and anything that has stood in a culvert go through the high one, which takes the glaze off a tile if a tile is left in it by mistake. Times are in the book, are held to the minute, and are not shortened for a fleet that wants a hull back before a weather window.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "The Refusal List"
    An article whose pieces cannot all take their oven is refused, and a refused article does not go back inside a wall. The list on the water door names oiled bearings, seated gaskets, closed voids that cannot be opened, laminated skins that delaminate at the middle heat, and sealed housings without a complete material schedule.

    Buyers for the enclaves read that door before they order, which is the whole of the shed's authority — it holds no office and answers to no enclave. #{ref :sterile_machines, "Machinery designed to come apart and be baked"} passes it as a matter of course, and the shed's masters will say so to a Chinese resistance supplier's face and to their own gate crews, who would rather buy nothing from that quarter at all.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Built on the creek careenage of a Grand Harbour dockyard, where a hull was hove down against the quay to have its bottom burned and scraped and the yard's book recorded what came off. Borrowed because it puts the whole operation on a slipway with a stripping floor beside it, and because the weighing-out that decides whether a piece is fitted back is the yard's own accounting rather than an inspection."
end
